### A.2 Standard Proximal Certificate and Firm Nonexpansiveness

Recall the convention
$$\operatorname{prox}_r(z) := \operatorname{argmin}_{u \in \mathbb{R}^d} \left\{ r(u) + \frac{1}{2}\|u - z\|^2 \right\}$$
for a proper ($\operatorname{dom} r := \{u \in \mathbb{R}^d : r(u) < +\infty\} \neq \emptyset$), lower-semicontinuous (lsc), and convex function $r: \mathbb{R}^d \to (-\infty, +\infty]$ (here $d = 2$). For every $z \in \mathbb{R}^d$, the objective $u \mapsto r(u) + \frac{1}{2}\|u - z\|^2$ is proper, lsc, and $1$-strongly convex (hence strictly convex and coercive), so it attains its minimum at a unique point $p = \operatorname{prox}_r(z) \in \operatorname{dom} r$.

#### Proposition A.1 (Three-Point Proximal Certificate and Firm Nonexpansiveness)
*Suppose an operator $T: \mathbb{R}^d \to \mathbb{R}^d$ admits the representation $T = \operatorname{prox}_r$ for a proper, lower-semicontinuous, convex function $r: \mathbb{R}^d \to (-\infty, +\infty]$. Then:*
1. *(Proximal Certificate / Three-Point Inequality) For every $z \in \mathbb{R}^d$, setting $p = T(z) \in \operatorname{dom} r$, and for every $u \in \operatorname{dom} r$,*
   $$r(p) + \frac{1}{2}\|p - z\|^2 + \frac{1}{2}\|u - p\|^2 \le r(u) + \frac{1}{2}\|u - z\|^2. \tag{A.12}$$
2. *(Firm Nonexpansiveness) For all $z, w \in \mathbb{R}^d$,*
   $$\|T(z) - T(w)\|^2 + \|(I - T)(z) - (I - T)(w)\|^2 \le \|z - w\|^2, \tag{A.13}$$
   *or equivalently,*
   $$\|T(z) - T(w)\|^2 \le \langle T(z) - T(w), z - w \rangle. \tag{A.14}$$
   *In particular, $T$ is nonexpansive: $\|T(z) - T(w)\| \le \|z - w\|$ for all $z, w \in \mathbb{R}^d$.*

#### Proof
1. **Proof of (A.12):** Fix $z \in \mathbb{R}^d$, let $p = T(z) = \operatorname{prox}_r(z) \in \operatorname{dom} r$, and let $u \in \operatorname{dom} r$. For any $\theta \in (0, 1)$, define the convex combination $u_\theta := (1 - \theta)p + \theta u = p + \theta(u - p) \in \operatorname{dom} r$. By optimality of $p$ and convexity of $r$,
   $$r(p) + \frac{1}{2}\|p - z\|^2 \le r(u_\theta) + \frac{1}{2}\|u_\theta - z\|^2 \le (1 - \theta)r(p) + \theta r(u) + \frac{1}{2}\|u_\theta - z\|^2.$$
   Using the exact Euclidean convex-combination identity
   $$\frac{1}{2}\|(1 - \theta)(p - z) + \theta(u - z)\|^2 = (1 - \theta)\frac{1}{2}\|p - z\|^2 + \theta \frac{1}{2}\|u - z\|^2 - \frac{\theta(1 - \theta)}{2}\|u - p\|^2,$$
   subtracting $(1 - \theta)\left(r(p) + \frac{1}{2}\|p - z\|^2\right)$ from both sides, and dividing by $\theta \in (0, 1)$ yields
   $$r(p) + \frac{1}{2}\|p - z\|^2 + \frac{1 - \theta}{2}\|u - p\|^2 \le r(u) + \frac{1}{2}\|u - z\|^2.$$
   Passing to the limit as $\theta \downarrow 0$ yields the proximal certificate (A.12).
   *(Equivalently, expanding $\frac{1}{2}\|u - z\|^2 = \frac{1}{2}\|u - p\|^2 + \frac{1}{2}\|p - z\|^2 + \langle u - p, p - z \rangle$ shows that (A.12) is identical to the subgradient optimality condition $r(u) \ge r(p) + \langle z - p, u - p \rangle$ for all $u \in \operatorname{dom} r$, i.e., $z - p \in \partial r(p)$.)*

2. **Proof of (A.13)–(A.14):** Let $z, w \in \mathbb{R}^d$, and set $p := T(z) \in \operatorname{dom} r$ and $q := T(w) \in \operatorname{dom} r$.
   - Applying (A.12) at input $z$ with test point $u = q \in \operatorname{dom} r$ gives
     $$r(p) + \frac{1}{2}\|p - z\|^2 + \frac{1}{2}\|q - p\|^2 \le r(q) + \frac{1}{2}\|q - z\|^2.$$
   - Applying (A.12) at input $w$ with test point $u = p \in \operatorname{dom} r$ gives
     $$r(q) + \frac{1}{2}\|q - w\|^2 + \frac{1}{2}\|p - q\|^2 \le r(p) + \frac{1}{2}\|p - w\|^2.$$
   Summing these two inequalities and canceling the finite terms $r(p) + r(q)$ produces
   $$\|p - q\|^2 \le \frac{1}{2}\left(\|q - z\|^2 - \|p - z\|^2 + \|p - w\|^2 - \|q - w\|^2\right).$$
   Applying the polarization identities
   $$\|q - z\|^2 - \|p - z\|^2 = \|p - q\|^2 + 2\langle q - p, p - z \rangle, \qquad \|p - w\|^2 - \|q - w\|^2 = \|p - q\|^2 + 2\langle p - q, q - w \rangle,$$
   the right-hand side simplifies to
   $$\frac{1}{2}\left(2\|p - q\|^2 + 2\langle p - q, (z - p) - (w - q) \rangle\right) = \langle p - q, z - w \rangle.$$
   Thus $\|p - q\|^2 \le \langle p - q, z - w \rangle$, which is (A.14). Finally, expanding
   $$\|(I - T)(z) - (I - T)(w)\|^2 = \|(z - w) - (p - q)\|^2 = \|z - w\|^2 - 2\langle p - q, z - w \rangle + \|p - q\|^2$$
   and substituting $2\langle p - q, z - w \rangle \ge 2\|p - q\|^2$ immediately gives (A.13). By Cauchy–Schwarz on (A.14), $\|T(z) - T(w)\|^2 \le \|T(z) - T(w)\|\|z - w\|$, so $\|T(z) - T(w)\| \le \|z - w\|$. $\blacksquare$

